#!/bin/bash
# wg-peer/help.loc - Module-specific help

cat <<'EOF'
Module: wg-peer - spoke side of a WireGuard network

  Configures a machine that dials OUT to a hub, holds the tunnel open through
  NAT, and re-resolves the hub's address when it changes.

  Generic. Nothing here is backup-specific. WHY a given network is configured
  the way it is lives in backup-box/README.md.

SUBCOMMANDS

  setup                  Install, generate a keypair, write the interface,
                         install the re-resolve timer, print the peer line.
      yamc -h BOX -e net=bkup -e addr=10.9.9.2 wg-peer
      yamc -h BOX -e net=bkup -e addr=10.9.9.2 -e yes=1 wg-peer

  status                 Interface, handshake age, DDNS drift, timer. Read-only.
      yamc -h BOX -e net=bkup wg-peer status

  -e net=NAME  REQUIRED, no default.
  -e addr=ADDR REQUIRED, no default. Without it the module lists the addresses
               already in use and stops - auto-picking risks a collision, and
               WireGuard does not error on overlapping AllowedIPs, it silently
               reassigns and misroutes the loser.

KEYS

  The private key is generated ON THIS MACHINE and never leaves it - not to
  yamc, not into yamc.local. Only the public key comes back, printed as a
  ready-to-paste line for yamc.local/wgnet/<net>.peers.

  That paste is manual by design: yamc modules do not write to yamc.local
  (AGENTS.md).

THE TWO /32s

  Address    = <this box>/32
  AllowedIPs = <hub>/32          the hub, and nothing else

  So this machine's routing table contains no route toward the hub's LAN at
  all. Combined with the matching /32 in the hub's peer stanza, isolation is
  structural at both ends rather than a firewall rule either side could get
  wrong.

  AllowedIPs = 0.0.0.0/0 would instead route ALL of this machine's traffic
  through the tunnel - correct for a laptop on hotel wifi, wrong for a remote
  box that should only be able to reach the hub.

ENDPOINT RE-RESOLUTION

  wg-quick resolves the endpoint hostname ONCE, at interface start. When a
  dynamic-DNS name changes address, the tunnel stays down until something
  restarts it. On an unattended box at a remote site that is the failure that
  matters most, and it is silent.

  So a systemd timer runs every 2 minutes and re-resolves ONLY when the
  handshake has actually gone stale (>150s), leaving a working tunnel alone:

    wg set bk0 peer <hub pubkey> endpoint bateman.cc:51822

  PersistentKeepalive=25 handles the other half - it holds the NAT mapping at
  the remote site open, and lets the hub learn this peer's current address.

  'wg-peer status' compares what the endpoint resolves to NOW against what the
  running tunnel is using, so DDNS drift is visible rather than mysterious.

MANUAL EQUIVALENT

    apt-get install wireguard-tools
    umask 077
    wg genkey | tee /etc/wireguard/bk0.key | wg pubkey > /etc/wireguard/bk0.pub

    cat > /etc/wireguard/bk0.conf <<'END'
    [Interface]
    Address    = 10.9.9.2/32
    PrivateKey = <contents of bk0.key>

    [Peer]
    PublicKey           = <hub public key>
    Endpoint            = bateman.cc:51822
    AllowedIPs          = 10.9.9.1/32
    PersistentKeepalive = 25
    END

    systemctl enable --now wg-quick@bk0
    cat /etc/wireguard/bk0.pub        # hand this to the hub operator

  Check:  wg show bk0     ('latest handshake' should be under ~2 minutes)
          ping 10.9.9.1   (the hub, and nothing beyond it)

INTERFACE NAMING

  Do not name it wg*. See 'wg-hub help' - the wg+ wildcard in common firewall
  snippets is the reason, and it applies to both ends. The module refuses.

SEE ALSO

  wg-hub                 the hub side
  backup-box/README.md   system design and threat model
EOF
